  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 318 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nPeriodic precipitation chances are the main forecast challenge \nin the long term forecast. An upper level low and trough will \nenter the northwestern portion of the CONUS Saturday night. \nDownstream, ridging will increase from eastern Wyoming into \ncentral Montana. Downstream of this ridge axis, mid level warm \nair advection will increase across South Dakota into northern \nNebraska late Saturday night into Sunday morning. The latest NBM\nensembles, with additional support from the deterministic GFS \nand EC solns develop precipitation late Saturday night across \nnorthern Nebraska and lift this to the east southeast into \nSunday morning. This precipitation follows a H700 frontal \nboundary from southern South Dakota into northern and \nnortheastern Nebraska. The latest NBM ensemble 12 hr QPF \nprobabilities have a 40 to 65 percent chance of exceeding 0.10\" \nof QPF east of a line from Valentine to Burwell ending at 1 PM \non Sunday. That being said, with support from the NBM and \ndeterministic GFS and EC solns, feel the low end likely pops in \nthe northeast FA are on track ATTM. The upper level low will \ntrack into the northern Rockies Sunday. Southerly winds will \nincrease downstream of this low and upper level trough axis from\nKansas northwest into western Nebraska and the northern high \nplains of Wyoming, the western Dakotas and eastern Montana. \nDecent low level moisture advection will commence Sunday across \nthe area. By afternoon, a plume of low level moisture surges \nnorth into western Nebraska and the panhandle. The degree of \nthis moisture is still up in the air with the deterministic \nsolns tonight with the GFS's surface dew points well into the \nlower 60s, while the NAM12 holds them back into the middle 50s. \nLate Sunday afternoon into the evening hours, convection looks \nto initiate over the Cheyenne Ridge and central Rockies. This \nactivity then lifts northeast Sunday night into Monday morning, \nmainly over western and northern Nebraska. The NBM initialized \nwith likely pops over the northern third of the forecast area \nSunday night and this appears on track considering the ensemble \nQPF forecast has a 50 to 75% chance of QPF's exceeding 0.10\" \nSunday night into Monday morning generally north of highway 2. \nAs for the severe threat Sunday night, a narrow theta e ridge \nnoses into the southern Panhandle Sunday afternoon and this is \nco-located with decent sfc to 6KM shear~40+kts. Any storms that \ndo initiate in this environment may be strong to severe \ninitially, but should weaken as they travel east of the \npanhandle. The upper level low and trough will track across \nsouth central Canada, approaching western Hudson Bay by Tuesday \nmorning. This feature will force a cold front through the \nforecast area Monday, stalling this feature out over northern \nKansas Tuesday into Wednesday. Precipitation chances will be \ngreatest Monday night across the southern forecast area INVOF \nthe front. This feature will stall out over Kansas Tuesday into \nWednesday with the best threat for precipitation off to the \nsouth of the forecast area. This front will then start to lift \nnorth Wednesday night into Thursday as an upper level trough \ntracks east southeast from the pacific NW into the northern \nRockies. This will lead to increasing chances for precipitation \nbeginning Thursday, carrying over Friday.",
